---A `transaction` object comprises contextual information associated with its respective event.
---This information is useful for understanding sequences of correlated events.
---For example, a `transaction` object such as the following:
---```
---{
---  "id": "Wn4f-0RQ8D8lTSLkAmkKdQAADqo",
---  "type": "WEB",
---  "detail": null
---}
---```
---indicates that a `WEB` request with `id` `Wn4f-0RQ8D8lTSLkAmkKdQAADqo` has created this event.
---
---A `transaction` object with a `requestApiTokenId` in the `detail` object, for example :
---```
---{
---  "id": "YjSlblAAqnKY7CdyCkXNBgAAAIU",
---  "type": "WEB",
---  "detail": {
---    "requestApiTokenId": "00T94e3cn9kSEO3c51s5"
---  }
---}
---```
---indicates that this event was the result of an action performed through an API using the token identified by 00T94e3cn9kSEO3c51s5. The token ID is visible in the Admin Console, **Security** > **API**. See [API token management](https://help.okta.com/okta_help.htm?id=Security_API). For more information on API tokens, see [Create an API token](https://developer.okta.com/docs/guides/create-an-api-token/).
---@class okta.LogTransaction
---@field detail? okta.LogTransactionDetailMap Details for this transaction.
---@field id? string Unique identifier for this transaction.
---@field type? string Describes the kind of transaction. `WEB` indicates a web request. `JOB` indicates an asynchronous task.

---Details for this transaction.
---@alias okta.LogTransactionDetailMap { [string]: any }

---"A user agent is software (a software agent) that is acting on behalf of a user." ([Definition of User Agent](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/User_agent))
---
---In the Okta event data object, the `UserAgent` object provides specifications about the client software that makes event-triggering HTTP requests. User agent identification is often useful for identifying interoperability problems between servers and clients, and also for browser and operating system usage analytics.
---@class okta.LogUserAgent
---@field browser? string If the client is a web browser, this field identifies the type of web browser (for example, CHROME, FIREFOX)
---@field os? string The operating system that the client runs on (for example, Windows 10)
---@field rawUserAgent? string A raw string representation of the user agent that is formatted according to [section 5.5.3 of HTTP/1.1 Semantics and Content](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7231#section-5.5.3). Both the `browser` and the `OS` fields can be derived from this field.

---The result of the user behavior detection models associated with the event
---@class okta.LogUserBehavior
---@field id? string|distilled.Null The unique identifier of the user behavior detection model
---@field name? string|distilled.Null The name of the user behavior detection model [configured by admins](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/Behavior/)
---@field result? okta.LogUserBehaviorResult|distilled.Null The result of the user behavior analysis

---The result of the user behavior analysis
---@alias okta.LogUserBehaviorResult "UNKNOWN"|"POSITIVE"|"NEGATIVE"|"BAD_REQUEST"

