---Specifies the behavior for establishing, validating, and matching a username for an IdP user
---@class okta.PolicySubject
---@field filter? string Optional [regular expression pattern](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Regular_expressions) used to filter untrusted IdP usernames. * As a best security practice, you should define a regular expression pattern to filter untrusted IdP usernames. This is especially important if multiple IdPs are connected to your org. The filter prevents an IdP from issuing an assertion for any user, including partners or directory users in your Okta org. * For example, the filter pattern `(\S+@example\.com)` allows only Users that have an `@example.com` username suffix. It rejects assertions that have any other suffix such as `@corp.example.com` or `@partner.com`. * Only `SAML2` and `OIDC` IdP providers support the `filter` property.
---@field matchAttribute? string Okta user profile attribute for matching a transformed IdP username. Only for matchType `CUSTOM_ATTRIBUTE`. The `matchAttribute` must be a valid Okta user profile attribute of one of the following types: * String (with no format or 'email' format only) * Integer * Number
---@field matchType? okta.PolicySubjectMatchType|string
---@field userNameTemplate? okta.PolicyUserNameTemplate

---Determines the Okta user profile attribute match conditions for account linking and authentication of the transformed IdP username
---@alias okta.PolicySubjectMatchType "CUSTOM_ATTRIBUTE"|"EMAIL"|"USERNAME"|"USERNAME_OR_EMAIL"

---All Okta orgs contain only one IdP discovery policy with an immutable default rule routing to your org's sign-in page. All Okta orgs also contain just one entity risk policy, one session protection policy, and one identity claims sourcing policy.
---@alias okta.PolicyType "ACCESS_POLICY"|"ENTITY_RISK"|"IDP_DISCOVERY"|"MFA_ENROLL"|"OKTA_SIGN_ON"|"PASSWORD"|"POST_AUTH_SESSION"|"PROFILE_ENROLLMENT"|"DEVICE_SIGNAL_COLLECTION"|"SESSION_VIOLATION_DETECTION"|"CLIENT_UPDATE"|"IDENTITY_CLAIM_SOURCING"

---@alias okta.PolicyTypeSimulation "ACCESS_POLICY"|"MFA_ENROLL"|"OKTA_SIGN_ON"|"PROFILE_ENROLLMENT"

---[Okta Expression Language (EL) expression](https://developer.okta.com/docs/reference/okta-expression-language/) to generate or transform a unique username for the IdP user.
---* IdP user profile attributes can be referenced with the `idpuser` prefix such as `idpuser.subjectNameId`.
---* You must define an IdP user profile attribute before it can be referenced in an Okta EL expression. To define an IdP user attribute policy, you may need to create a new IdP instance without a base profile property. Then edit the IdP user profile to update the IdP instance with an expression that references the IdP user profile attribute that you just created.
---@class okta.PolicyUserNameTemplate
---@field template? string

---@class okta.PossessionConstraint
---@field authenticationMethods? okta.PossessionConstraintAuthenticationMethodsList This property specifies the precise authenticator and method for authentication. <x-lifecycle class="oie"></x-lifecycle>
---@field excludedAuthenticationMethods? any This property specifies the precise authenticator and method to exclude from authentication. <x-lifecycle class="oie"></x-lifecycle>
---@field methods? okta.PossessionConstraintMethodsList The authenticator methods that are permitted
---@field reauthenticateIn? string The duration after which the user must re-authenticate regardless of user activity. This re-authentication interval overrides the Verification Method object's `reauthenticateIn` interval. The supported values use ISO 8601 period format for recurring time intervals (for example, `PT1H`).
---@field required? boolean This property indicates whether the knowledge or possession factor is required by the assurance. It's optional in the request, but is always returned in the response. By default, this field is `true`. If the knowledge or possession constraint has values for `excludedAuthenticationMethods` the `required` value is false. <x-lifecycle class="oie"></x-lifecycle>
---@field types? okta.PossessionConstraintTypesList The authenticator types that are permitted
---@field deviceBound? okta.PossessionConstraintDeviceBound|string Indicates if device-bound Factors are required. This property is only set for `POSSESSION` constraints.
---@field hardwareProtection? okta.PossessionConstraintHardwareProtection|string Indicates if any secrets or private keys used during authentication must be hardware protected and not exportable. This property is only set for `POSSESSION` constraints.
---@field phishingResistant? okta.PossessionConstraintPhishingResistant|string Indicates if phishing-resistant Factors are required. This property is only set for `POSSESSION` constraints.
---@field userPresence? okta.PossessionConstraintUserPresence|string Indicates if the user needs to approve an Okta Verify prompt or provide biometrics (meets NIST AAL2 requirements). This property is only set for `POSSESSION` constraints.
---@field userVerification? okta.PossessionConstraintUserVerification|string Indicates the user interaction requirement (PIN or biometrics) to ensure verification of a possession factor
---@field userVerificationMethods? okta.PossessionConstraintUserVerificationMethodsList Indicates which methods can be used for user verification. `userVerificationMethods` can only be used when `userVerification` is `REQUIRED`. `BIOMETRICS` is currently the only supported method.

