---@class okta.UserOutput
---@field activated? string|distilled.Null The timestamp when the user status transitioned to `ACTIVE`
---@field created? string The timestamp when the user was created
---@field credentials? okta.UserCredentialsOutput
---@field id? string The unique key for the user
---@field lastLogin? string|distilled.Null The timestamp of the last login
---@field lastUpdated? string The timestamp when the user was last updated
---@field passwordChanged? distilled.Secret|distilled.Null The timestamp when the user's password was last updated
---@field profile? okta.UserProfile
---@field realmId? string The ID of the realm in which the user is residing. See [Realms](/openapi/okta-management/management/tags/realm).
---@field status? okta.UserStatus
---@field statusChanged? string|distilled.Null The timestamp when the status of the user last changed
---@field transitioningToStatus? okta.UserOutputTransitioningToStatus|distilled.Null The target status of an in-progress asynchronous status transition. This property is only returned if the user's state is transitioning.
---@field type? okta.UserOutputType The user type that determines the schema for the user's profile. The `type` property is a map that identifies the [User Types](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/UserType/#tag/UserType). Currently it contains a single element, `id`. It can be specified when creating a new user, and can be updated by an admin on a full replace of an existing user (but not a partial update).
---@field _embedded? okta.UserOutputEmbeddedMap Embedded resources related to the user using the [JSON Hypertext Application Language](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-kelly-json-hal-06) specification
---@field _links? okta.UserOutputLinks Specifies link relations (see [Web Linking](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8288) available for the current status of a user. The links object is used for dynamic discovery of related resources, lifecycle operations, and credential operations. The links object is read-only. For an individual user result, the links object contains a full set of link relations available for that user as determined by your policies. For a collection of users, the links object contains only the `self` link. Operations that return a collection of users include [List all users](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/user/listusers) and [List all group member users](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/Group/#tag/Group/operation/listGroupUsers).

