---The target status of an in-progress asynchronous status transition. This property is only returned if the user's state is transitioning.
---@alias okta.UserOutputTransitioningToStatus "ACTIVE"|"DEPROVISIONED"|"PROVISIONED"

---The user type that determines the schema for the user's profile. The `type` property is a map that identifies the [User Types](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/UserType/#tag/UserType).
---
---Currently it contains a single element, `id`. It can be specified when creating a new user, and can be updated by an admin on a full replace of an existing user (but not a partial update).
---@class okta.UserOutputType
---@field id? string The ID of the user type

---Specifies the default and custom profile properties for a user.
---
---The default user profile is based on the [System for Cross-domain Identity Management: Core Schema](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7643).
---
---The only permitted customizations of the default profile are to update permissions, change whether the `firstName` and `lastName` properties are nullable, and specify a [pattern](https://developer.okta.com/docs/reference/api/schemas/#login-pattern-validation) for `login`. You can use the Profile Editor in the Admin Console or the [Schemas API](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/UISchema/#tag/UISchema) to make schema modifications.
---
---You can extend user profiles with custom properties. You must first add the custom property to the user profile schema before you reference it.
---You can use the Profile Editor in the Admin Console or the [Schemas API](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/UISchema/#tag/UISchema) to manage schema extensions.
---
---Custom attributes can contain HTML tags. It's the client's responsibility to escape or encode this data before displaying it. Use [best-practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html) to prevent cross-site scripting.
---@class okta.UserProfile
---@field city? string|distilled.Null The city or locality of the user's address (`locality`)
---@field costCenter? string|distilled.Null Name of the cost center assigned to a user
---@field countryCode? string|distilled.Null The country name component of the user's address (`country`). For validation, see [ISO 3166-1 alpha 2 "short" code format](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-scim-core-schema-22#ref-ISO3166).
---@field department? string Name of the user's department
---@field displayName? string|distilled.Null Name of the user suitable for display to end users
---@field division? string|distilled.Null Name of the user's division
---@field email? string The primary email address of the user. For validation, see [RFC 5322 Section 3.2.3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5322#section-3.2.3).
---@field employeeNumber? string The organization or company assigned unique identifier for the user
---@field firstName? string|distilled.Null Given name of the user (`givenName`)
---@field honorificPrefix? string|distilled.Null Honorific prefix(es) of the user, or title in most Western languages
---@field honorificSuffix? string|distilled.Null Honorific suffix(es) of the user
---@field lastName? string|distilled.Null The family name of the user (`familyName`)
---@field locale? string The user's default location for purposes of localizing items such as currency, date time format, numerical representations, and so on. A locale value is a concatenation of the ISO 639-1 two-letter language code, an underscore, and the ISO 3166-1 two-letter country code. For example, en_US specifies the language English and country US. This value is `en_US` by default.
---@field login? string The unique identifier for the user (`username`). For validation, see [Login pattern validation](https://developer.okta.com/docs/reference/api/schemas/#login-pattern-validation). Every user within your Okta org must have a unique identifier for a login. This constraint applies to all users you import from other systems or applications such as Active Directory. Your organization is the top-level namespace to mix and match logins from all your connected applications or directories. Careful consideration of naming conventions for your login identifier will make it easier to onboard new applications in the future. Logins are not considered unique if they differ only in case and/or diacritical marks. If one of your users has a login of `Isaac.Brock@example.com`, there cannot be another user whose login is `isaac.brock@example.com`, nor `isáàc.bröck@example.com`. Okta has a default ambiguous name resolution policy for usernames that include @-signs. By default, usernames must be formatted as email addresses and thus always include @-signs. You can remove that restriction using either the Admin Console or the [Schemas API](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/Schema/). Users can sign in with their non-qualified short name (for example: `isaac.brock` with username `isaac.brock@example.com`) as long as the short name is still unique within the org.
---@field manager? string|distilled.Null The `displayName` of the user's manager
---@field managerId? string|distilled.Null The `id` of the user's manager
---@field middleName? string|distilled.Null The middle name of the user
---@field mobilePhone? string|distilled.Null The mobile phone number of the user
---@field nickName? string|distilled.Null The casual way to address the user in real life
---@field organization? string|distilled.Null Name of the the user's organization
---@field postalAddress? string|distilled.Null Mailing address component of the user's address
---@field preferredLanguage? string|distilled.Null The user's preferred written or spoken language. For validation, see [RFC 7231 Section 5.3.5](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7231#section-5.3.5).
---@field primaryPhone? string|distilled.Null The primary phone number of the user such as a home number
---@field profileUrl? string|distilled.Null The URL of the user's online profile. For example, a web page. See [URL](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1808).
---@field secondEmail? string|distilled.Null The secondary email address of the user typically used for account recovery. For validation, see [RFC 5322 Section 3.2.3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5322#section-3.2.3).
---@field state? string|distilled.Null The state or region component of the user's address (`region`)
---@field streetAddress? string|distilled.Null The full street address component of the user's address
---@field timezone? string|distilled.Null The user's time zone
---@field title? string|distilled.Null The user's title, such as Vice President
---@field userType? string|distilled.Null The property used to describe the organization-to-user relationship, such as employee or contractor > **Note:** The `userType` property is a standard string attribute and should be treated as a descriptive label rather than a functional setting. Unlike a user's assigned user [`type`](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/User/#tag/User/operation/createUser!path=type&t=request), this property is purely informational and doesn't influence the behavior of the Okta platform.
---@field zipCode? string|distilled.Null The ZIP code or postal code component of the user's address (`postalCode`)
