---All Okta-defined profile properties are defined in a profile subschema with the resolution scope `#base`. You can't modify these properties, except to update permissions, to change the nullability of `firstName` and `lastName`, or to specify a pattern for `login`. They can't be removed.
---
---The base user profile is based on the [System for Cross-domain Identity Management: Core Schema](https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-scim-core-schema-22#section-4.1.1) and has the standard properties detailed below.
---@class okta.UserSchemaBaseInput
---@field properties? okta.UserSchemaBaseProperties The `#base` object properties

---@class okta.UserSchemaBaseProperties
---@field city? okta.UserSchemaAttribute City or locality component of the user's address (`locality`)
---@field costCenter? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Name of a cost center assigned to the user
---@field countryCode? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Country name component of the user's address (`country`.) This property uses [ISO 3166-1 alpha 2 "short" code format](https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-scim-core-schema-22#ref-ISO3166).
---@field department? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Name of the user's department
---@field displayName? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Name of the user, suitable for display to end users
---@field division? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Name of the user's division
---@field email? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Primary email address of the user. This property is formatted according to [RFC 5322 Section 3.2.3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5322#section-3.2.3).
---@field employeeNumber? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Organization or company assigned unique identifier for the user
---@field firstName? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Given name of the user (`givenName`)
---@field honorificPrefix? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Honorific prefix(es) of the user or title in most Western languages
---@field honorificSuffix? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Honorific suffix(es) of the user
---@field lastName? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Family name of the user (`familyName`)
---@field locale? okta.UserSchemaAttribute User's default location for purposes of localizing items such as currency, date time format, numerical representations, and so on. A locale value is a concatenation of the ISO 639-1 two-letter language code, an underscore, and the ISO 3166-1 two-letter country code. For example: `en_US` specifies the language English and country US. This value is `en_US` by default.
---@field login? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Unique identifier for the user (`userName`) The login property is validated according to its pattern attribute, which is a string. By default, the attribute is null. When the attribute is null, the username is required to be formatted as an email address as defined by [RFC 6531 Section 3.3](http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc6531#section-3.3). The pattern can be set through the API to one of the following forms. (The Admin Console provides access to the same forms.) * A login pattern of `".+"` indicates that there is no restriction on usernames. Any non-empty, unique value is permitted, and the minimum length of five isn't enforced. In this case, usernames don't need to include the `@` character. If a name does include `@`, the portion ahead of the `@` can be used for logging in, provided it identifies a unique user within the org. * A login pattern of the form `"[...]+"` indicates that usernames must only contain characters from the set given between the brackets. The enclosing brackets and final `+` are required for this form. Character ranges can be indicated using hyphens. To include the hyphen itself in the allowed set, the hyphen must appear first. Any characters in the set except the hyphen, a-z, A-Z, and 0-9 must be preceded by a backslash (`\`). For example, `"[a-z13579\.]+"` would restrict usernames to lowercase letters, odd digits, and periods, while `"[-a-zA-Z0-9]+"` would allow basic alphanumeric characters and hyphens.
---@field manager? okta.UserSchemaAttribute The `displayName` of the user's manager
---@field managerId? okta.UserSchemaAttribute The `id` of the user's manager
---@field middleName? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Middle name(s) of the user
---@field mobilePhone? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Mobile phone number of the user
---@field nickName? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Casual way to address the user in real life
---@field organization? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Name of the user's organization
---@field postalAddress? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Mailing address component of the user's address
---@field preferredLanguage? okta.UserSchemaAttribute User's preferred written or spoken languages. This property is formatted according to [RFC 7231 Section 5.3.5](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7231#section-5.3.5).
---@field primaryPhone? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Primary phone number of the user, such as home number
---@field profileUrl? okta.UserSchemaAttribute URL of the user's online profile (for example, a web page.) This property is formatted according to the [Relative Uniform Resource Locators specification](https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-scim-core-schema-22#ref-ISO3166).
---@field secondEmail? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Secondary email address of the user typically used for account recovery. This property is formatted according to [RFC 5322 Section 3.2.3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5322#section-3.2.3).
---@field state? okta.UserSchemaAttribute State or region component of the user's address (`region`)
---@field streetAddress? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Full street address component of the user's address
---@field timezone? okta.UserSchemaAttribute User's time zone. This property is formatted according to the [IANA Time Zone database format](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc6557).
---@field title? okta.UserSchemaAttribute User's title, such as "Vice President"
---@field userType? okta.UserSchemaAttribute Used to describe the organization to the user relationship such as "Employee" or "Contractor". **Note:** The `userType` field is an arbitrary string value and isn't related to the newer [User Types](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/UserType/) feature.
---@field zipCode? okta.UserSchemaAttribute ZIP code or postal code component of the user's address (`postalCode`)

